// Package routing is goncini's router: Symfony's Routing component, on
// net/http's ServeMux.
//
// Routes are registered in plain Go, and can be named, grouped under a
// prefix, and wrapped in middleware:
//
//	r := routing.New()
//	r.Get("/articles/{slug}", httpkernel.Endpoint(c.Show)).Name("article_show")
//	admin := r.Group("/admin").NamePrefix("admin_").With(requireAdmin)
//	admin.Delete("/articles/{slug}", httpkernel.Endpoint(c.Delete)).Name("article_delete")
//
// A Router is an http.Handler. Behind an httpkernel.Kernel, requests that
// match no route get 404 and 405 problems.
//
// The parts of an app that have routes, such as controllers, implement
// Routes, and New or Include registers them.
//
// # Paths
//
// Paths are ServeMux patterns without the method and host. A {name}
// wildcard matches one segment, never empty, and a final {name...} wildcard
// the rest of the path, possibly empty. Handlers read them with
// r.PathValue, or with path tags in an httpkernel.Endpoint input. A GET
// route also answers HEAD requests.
//
// One thing differs from ServeMux: a route matches its path exactly.
// ServeMux takes a pattern ending in a slash, such as /articles/, to match
// every path below it as well; a route with that path matches /articles/
// only, though ServeMux still redirects /articles to it. To match a
// subtree, end the path with a {name...} wildcard: /files/{path...}.
//
// Registering a route panics, saying where, if its path isn't one that
// ServeMux can route: one with a double slash, or a . or .. segment. Two
// routes that would match the same requests, with neither more specific
// than the other, are a conflict: registering the second one panics, and
// says where both were registered.
//
// # Requirements
//
// As in Symfony, a wildcard can require its value to match a regular
// expression, in the path or with Require; constants such as Digits and
// UUID hold common ones:
//
//	r.Get("/articles/{id<\d+>}", httpkernel.Endpoint(c.ShowByID))
//	r.Get("/articles/{slug}", httpkernel.Endpoint(c.Show)).Require("slug", routing.ASCIISlug)
//
// A route matches only the requests that meet its requirements. Routes with
// the same method and path, but for the names of their wildcards, are tried
// in the order they were registered, so /articles/12 goes to the first route
// above and /articles/hello-world to the second; a request that meets none
// gets a 404. Every route but the last of such routes needs requirements,
// or the next one could never match: registering it panics, as a conflict.
// Routes with different paths are still told apart by ServeMux, and a
// request that a route matches but doesn't meet the requirements of gets a
// 404, not another route.
//
// # URLs
//
// A Route builds its URLs from its parameters, so that paths are written
// once, in the routes. A controller keeps the routes it links to as it
// registers them, which no typo can break:
//
//	func (c *Controller) Routes(r *routing.Router) {
//		c.show = r.Get("/articles/{slug}", httpkernel.Endpoint(c.Show)).Name("article_show")
//	}
//
//	loc := must c.show.URL(routing.Params{"slug": "hello-world"}) // /articles/hello-world
//
// Code that doesn't register the route finds it by name instead, with
// Router.URL: r.URL("article_show", params). The URL is relative to the
// host; httpkernel.BaseURL gives the scheme and host for an absolute one.
//
// Parameters that aren't wildcards of the route go into the query string.
// Each value is escaped, so that the route gets back exactly the value
// given, a value must meet its wildcard's requirement, and URL fails rather
// than return a URL that another route would serve. The URLError cases say what went wrong; they are mistakes in
// the code that builds the URL, so a handler can panic on them with must.
//
// # Listing
//
// List returns the routes in the order they were registered, and
// WriteTable prints them the way debug:router does. Match tells which route
// serves a request.
package routing
